    "is_free": false,
    "detailed_description": "<p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>ARIJIGOKU</strong> is a short, 2D pixel art <i>point-and-click</i> adventure horror game where you control <strong>Erik</strong>, a man searching for meaning in the aftermath of personal tragedy.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >Inspired by classic adventure games, you'll explore a secluded forest, solve environmental puzzles, and use found items to move the story forward. The game uses a simple two-click control scheme:</p><ul class=\"bb_ul\"><li><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Left click</strong> to <strong>Interact</strong></p></li><li><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Right click</strong> to <strong>Examine</strong></p></li></ul><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >While the path is mostly linear, there are <strong>three possible endings</strong>, and a few decisions (or careless actions) that can lead to Erik\u2019s death.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Save often. Curiosity has consequences.</strong></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><ul class=\"bb_ul\"><li><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Playtime:</strong> Around 25 minutes for experienced players; longer if you're exploring or get stuck.</p></li></ul><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><ul class=\"bb_ul\"><li><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong><i>Includes a full interactive tutorial, accessible from the main menu.</i></strong></p></li></ul><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >STORY DESCRIPTION:\u00a0</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >Erik has lost everything: his daughter, his wife, and now, his will to keep going. Desperate to escape the weight of his grief, he drives aimlessly northward, seeking nothing but distance.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >When his car breaks down on a remote stretch of Northern Ontario highway, he wanders into the woods looking for help. What he finds is something else entirely... something that will change him forever.</p>",
    "about_the_game": "<p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>ARIJIGOKU</strong> is a short, 2D pixel art <i>point-and-click</i> adventure horror game where you control <strong>Erik</strong>, a man searching for meaning in the aftermath of personal tragedy.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >Inspired by classic adventure games, you'll explore a secluded forest, solve environmental puzzles, and use found items to move the story forward. The game uses a simple two-click control scheme:</p><ul class=\"bb_ul\"><li><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Left click</strong> to <strong>Interact</strong></p></li><li><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Right click</strong> to <strong>Examine</strong></p></li></ul><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >While the path is mostly linear, there are <strong>three possible endings</strong>, and a few decisions (or careless actions) that can lead to Erik\u2019s death.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Save often. Curiosity has consequences.</strong></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><ul class=\"bb_ul\"><li><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Playtime:</strong> Around 25 minutes for experienced players; longer if you're exploring or get stuck.</p></li></ul><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><ul class=\"bb_ul\"><li><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong><i>Includes a full interactive tutorial, accessible from the main menu.</i></strong></p></li></ul><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >STORY DESCRIPTION:\u00a0</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >Erik has lost everything: his daughter, his wife, and now, his will to keep going. Desperate to escape the weight of his grief, he drives aimlessly northward, seeking nothing but distance.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >When his car breaks down on a remote stretch of Northern Ontario highway, he wanders into the woods looking for help. What he finds is something else entirely... something that will change him forever.</p>",
    "short_description": "A short, narrative-driven 2D pixel art adventure/horror game. Play as Erik, a man lost in grief, as you explore a secluded forest, solve puzzles, and uncover the truth. With multiple endings and deadly choices, curiosity can cost you \u2014 so save often.",
